==English==
{{wikipedia|Biomass (ecology)|mul=Biomass}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|bio-|mass}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbaɪ.ə(ʊ)ˌmæs/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-biomass.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|bi|o|mass}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# The total mass of a [[living]] thing or part thereof, such as a [[cell]].
#* '''2005''', David A Guertin and David M Sabatini, chapter "Cell Size Control" in ''Encyclopedia of Life Sciences'', Wiley:
#*: Since protein constitutes the majority of the '''biomass''' of a cell, building new protein is a major way that cells increase their size.
# The total mass of all [[living]] things within a specific area, [[habitat]] etc.
#* '''2015''', Theo Tait, ‘Don't wear yum-yum yellow’, ''London Review of Books'', vol. 34 no. 15:
#*: In the untouched Shangri-la-like Kingman Reef in the Line Islands in the central Pacific, sharks make up 75 per cent of the fish '''biomass'''.
# [[vegetation|Vegetation]] used as a [[fuel]], or source of [[energy]], especially if [[cultivated]] for that purpose.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1997|author=R. Socolow|title=Industrial Ecology and Global Change|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=9780521577830|page=199
|passage=Advanced technologies such as gasifier/gas turbine systems for electric power generation and fuel cells for transportation make it possible for '''biomass''' to provide a substantial share of world energy in the decades ahead, at competitive costs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 April 22|author=Paul Stephen|title=COVID-19: meet the railway heroes|journal=Rail|page=41|text=Among the key contracts she works on is the supply of coal and '''biomass''' to [[Drax]] power station and extensive rail operations for British Steel.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2020 August 26|author=Andrew Mourant|title=Reinforced against future flooding|journal=Rail|pages=60-61|text=All vegetation and tree stumps were sent to a local recycling facility to be turned into '''biomass'''.}}

